+++ b/docs/workspace-shared-tools.md
@@ -0,0 +1,165 @@
+# Workspace shared tools and isolated agent runtimes
+
+**Design + first backend implementation, 25 October 2026.** Applies to the Potbot
+workspace sandbox (called a *repository* in the product UI), not to an individual
+Git checkout. Based on `main` at `7bc8794615426ffeb6e8619ba97d60c2d02fb51c`.
+
+## Contract
+
+Each workspace has one shared, group-writable installation library presented to
+approved sandbox processes as `/usr/local/tools`. Every agent has its own home,
+private writable runtime and Git checkouts. `/home/user/Tools` is an absolute
+**guest** symlink to `/usr/local/tools`, not a host-side link to arbitrary `/usr`.
+
+```text
+Host: <registered-workspace-root>/
+  tools/                                   <- group owner = this workspace only, 2770
+    <tool>/
+      checkout/                            <- pinned, clean Git tree (no runtime files)
+      downloads/                           <- immutable reviewed wheel/tarball archives
+      build/                               <- compiled outputs, ABI-tagged
+      environment/                         <- reusable, frozen venv/node_modules/etc.
+  environments/agents/
+    <agent-id>/home/                       <- private, 0700
+      Tools -> /usr/local/tools            <- resolves inside sandbox only
+      Projects/<tool>/                     <- independent editable clone
+      .cache/tools/<tool>/                 <- per-agent cache
+      .local/state/tools/<tool>/           <- per-agent state
+      .local/tmp/tools/<tool>/             <- per-agent scratch
+  models/                                  <- mutable models mapped to /var/ai
+  receipts/shared-tool-manifests/          <- controller-owned recipe, executable digest
+  receipts/shared-tool-locks/              <- controller-owned reader/writer lease files
+
+Guest per agent:
+  /usr/local/tools -> workspace's shared tools/ mount
+  /home/user       -> environments/agents/<id>/home bind mount
+  /home/user/Tools -> /usr/local/tools
+  /var/ai          -> workspace's writable models/ mount
+```
+
+**Precondition:** The qualified base image/WSL distro must create empty
+`/usr/local/tools`, `/home/user` and `/var/ai` mountpoints *before* Bubblewrap
+runs. The host's `/usr` is mounted read-only. No agent may mount the host's actual
+`/usr/local/tools`. Linux Bubblewrap cannot safely make `/usr/local/tools` under
+an already read-only `/usr`; the modified launcher rejects absent mountpoints.
+Provision the workspace group owner and setgid directory permissions explicitly;
+`2770` alone does not set the correct group identity. Agent home owner/ACL must
+remain private even though the tools library is shared. Do not make the shared
+library world-writable (never mode `777`). The operator must ensure mountpoint
+and filesystem permissions as part of image qualification.
+
+## Tool installation and selection
+
+The trusted, operator/controller-mediated API is
+`potbot.tools.shared_installations.SharedToolStore` (do **not** expose it as a
+model-selectable shell command):
+
+1. **Discover:** Validate a reviewed GitHub source URL, exact 40-character commit,
+   launcher relative path, platform/ABI tag, expected model-download behaviour,
+   and required destinations under `/var/ai`.
+2. **Reuse:** For an already installed tool, `use(tool, abi_tag=...)` acquires a
+   shared lease, verifies its pinned revision, ignored/untracked file cleanliness,
+   launcher digest and ABI, and returns the launcher plus frozen environment.
+   Retain the context manager through the **whole** supervised invocation.
+   Ordinary agents get a **read-only** tools mount; execution remains subject to
+   the existing controller ToolDefinition/CapabilityContext/lease/budget/pinned
+   executable enforcement. Merely finding a binary never authorizes execution.
+3. **First install:** `stage(recipe, admitted_local_source)` copies a clean,
+   pinned, local Git repository (without hardlinks). The *approved sandboxed
+   installer* populates `downloads/`, `build/`, and `environment/`; the caller
+   qualifies relocatability, architecture and runtime pinning. `register(recipe)`
+   publishes the manifest *outside* the writable tool directory. Scripts and
+   network access are **not** executed by the operator API itself. Use only
+   operator-approved installed paths for real process execution.
+4. **Edit/upgrade:** `fork_for_edit(tool, trusted_agent_id)` obtains an exclusive
+   lease, `reset --hard` and `clean -ffdx` the shared source back to its exact
+   pinned SHA, verifies the published tool, then clones a detached independent
+   checkout into `/home/user/Projects/<tool>` without hardlinks/alternates.
+   Edits, builds and tests happen in that agent checkout and private state;
+   there is no live in-place mutation of the shared tool. Promoting upgrades
+   needs a separately reviewed, version-pinned install, validation and publish
+   transaction—not a blind `git pull` on the shared checkout.
+5. **Clean:** Every registered tool has the mandatory generic `clean(tool)`
+   routine; no tool-specific cleanup script is blindly trusted. It discards
+   tracked modifications, untracked and ignored files under the **shared Git
+   checkout only** and verifies pinned revision + clean status. `downloads/`,
+   `build/` and `environment/` are siblings and are preserved. For a specific
+   agent, `clean_agent_runtime(tool, agent_id)` deletes only that agent's
+   `.cache`, `.local/state`, and `.local/tmp` tool-specific directories and
+   rejects symlink traversal. It intentionally never deletes another agent's
+   cache, checkout, or credentials. Both operations are lock-coordinated.
+
+### Artifact classification — what can really be shared?
+
+| Artifact | Shared read-only at execution? | Storage | Qualification |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Pinned Git source checkout | Yes | `/usr/local/tools/<tool>/checkout` | exact commit, clean tree, separate edit clone |
+| Package archives/wheels/npm tarballs/crates | Yes | `downloads/` | checksum/SBOM/license checks, offline reproducibility; installer-only writes |
+| Native binaries, `.so`, compiled wheels, wasm | Yes, for matching platform | `build/` | arch/libc/CPU/runtime ABI tag; no host-specific absolute links |
+| Python venv, node modules, vendor dependencies | Conditionally | `environment/` | frozen lockfile, relocation or fixed path, ABI match, no auto-sync or writes during execution |
+| Pip/uv/npm/cargo build caches, compiler scratch | No (mutable) | `/home/user/.cache` or `.local/tmp` | per-agent cache/locks, no shared mutable cache reuse |
+| Logs, PID files, temporary files, sockets, X sessions | No | per-agent home/state/tmp | never written in checkout or static environment |
+| Home config, tokens, SSH/Git credentials, browser profiles | No | private agent home/controller broker | never publish in shared artifacts |
+| ML model weights, tokenizer files | Qualified shared global read-only and/or writable workspace area | global mounted model store and `/var/ai` | declared model download gate, content identification, immutable links, controlled writable staging |
+| GPU runtime cache, Torch JIT cache, per-process kernels | No by default | per-agent cache | host/device/driver-specific, avoid concurrent corruption |
+| Editable tool fork | No | `/home/user/Projects/<tool>` | independent checkout; no hardlinks to shared install |
+
+For Python, prefer building/installing a **wheelhouse** once and installing to
+an ABI-qualified *fixed path* environment, then launch the environment's actual
+entrypoint directly. Avoid `uv run` or `pip install` auto-mutating a frozen shared
+environment: use `uv run --no-sync` only where verified. For Node, install
+lockfile-pinned `node_modules` with `npm ci` in the installer, then run `node`
+directly; leave npm cache per-agent. For Rust/Go/C/C++, share deterministic
+outputs only when target triple, runtime libraries, compiler flags and ABI match.
+For GPU binaries include driver/CUDA/runtime constraints in the qualified tag.
+
+**Model download gate:** installation recipes explicitly declare whether tools
+fetch models and a list of allowed `/var/ai/...` destinations. An installer
+must audit/cache-redirection of Hugging Face, Transformers, PyTorch, Ollama, and
+tool-specific custom downloaders; generic declared paths do not prove an
+arbitrary installer obeyed them. Actual filesystem monitoring and global
+read-only model-store import remain separate operator deliverables.
+
+## Security and concurrency
+
+- A tool checkout is a *shared untrusted asset*, not an access-control boundary.
+  `ToolDefinition` pinning and the existing controller admission remain mandatory.
+  Published manifest and lockfiles are outside the group-writable tools tree.
+- The consumer process gets the tools mount **read-only**. Only an expressly
+  authorized installer process may set `shared_tools_write=True`. Group-writable
+  host ownership is not an authorization to bypass the controller.
+- `use` holds a reader `flock`; clean, new staging, and editable fork take a
+  writer `flock`. All controller operations on a given tool must honor it.
+  The lock does **not** prevent a malicious agent that has obtained unrelated
+  writable mount access or an external host process from tampering mid-run.
+  Deployment must restrict writable mounts to admitted installer lifetimes and
+  qualify stronger immutable/content-addressed snapshots where adversarial
+  co-tenants must be defended against.
+- Cross-workspace paths, symlinked roots, missing mounts, incorrect ABI,
+  missing pinned checkout, and dirty shared source fail closed. Agents never
+  receive writable parent mounts (`environments/` or `tools/`) merely to execute
+  an existing tool. Linux/WSL2 is the initial qualified target, not native
+  Windows/macOS sandbox execution.
+- Workspace-relative host source identity is bound to trusted paths. The
+  controller must establish GitHub source provenance and installer approval;
+  passing a URL string in a recipe **does not** verify the network source.
+
+## Status and follow-on gates
+
+**Implemented in this increment:** agent home layout and alias, guest environment,
+optional agent-aware Bubblewrap mounts via ProcessRequest, opt-in controller/CLI
+adapters, pinned shared install catalogue, generic source cleaning, independent
+agent fork, model redirection declaration, and offline real-Git unit tests.
+Existing legacy tools keep their old launch profile until a qualified base image
+and trusted controller opt-in are provisioned; a patch alone does not enable
+mounting or installation automatically in deployed workspaces.
+
+**Required before production qualification:** (a) provision workspace Unix groups,
+image mountpoints and per-agent POSIX identities; (b) integrate the controller's
+install/upgrade approval with this catalogue and durable receipts; (c) tie the
+shared use lease to each full supervised process lifetime; (d) add actual
+installer network/egress/model-write mediation and SBOM/hash reproducibility
+and ABI/relocation test gates; (e) exercise real Linux Bubblewrap/Xpra under
+concurrent agents with negative cross-workspace/host-symlink tests; (f) add
+failure-atomic staged upgrades and rollback. Do not label the opt-in profile
+production-qualified until those checks pass.
